;; REGISTERED 2026-10-16 - orchestration PHASE 1
;; (docs/handoffs/orchestrator-recon-D3-phased-plan.md). The commit that
;; registers this chapter in pricklypear.borg ALSO lands the
;; borge/workstreams.borg obituary superseding the 2026-09-26
;; sessions-as-runs stance FOR SIDEcar RUNS - one commit, never ratified
;; later (gregor review point 1, binding). Provenance: drafted from the
;; orchestration recon (D1 findings + D2 draft, pushed wyo 7f2f15d); GREGOR
;; REVIEW 2026-10-16: RATIFY - decided points are folded inline as RATIFIED;
;; items still owned by roerick are marked OPEN with the proposed default
;; recorded. Phase 1 ships THIS SPEC ONLY - no code (D3).

(project pricklypear-run-primitive
 (doc "The run primitive - durable, chunked, resumable execution rows for
   the PP orchestrator. One run = one attempt to advance one card one gate,
   executed by a SUPERVISED SIDECAR RUNNER outside the PP runtime process,
   with state cheap enough to abandon and expensive enough to resume. The
   supervisor (a glochid app, phase 2) owns claims, budgets, transitions,
   and evidence; PP rows are the queue; the LLM loop NEVER runs inside the
   PP runtime process (the absolute rule, jobs.borg:246-253 + gregor review
   point 5, including the phase-3 native runner).")
 (status planned)

 (* agent note (|PROVENANCE + RATIFICATION (2026-10-16): drafted from the
     orchestration recon (D1 findings, D2 draft) and gregor's review.
     DECIDED (gregor, D4): storage = shared-space datum data.s.run.* +
     single supervisor + ONE CAS on queued->claimed (the reap-takeover is
     already a two-supervisor moment - a booting supervisor racing one that
     is stalled, not dead); usage from executor terminal output is a
     SELF-REPORT - the budget gate is ADVISORY until provider-structured
     usage lands; POLICY-2 backoff = the jobs shape (2^n capped 60s);
     POLICY-3 keeps the NO-VERDICT fail-safe verbatim; concurrency default
     2 until the overnight soak passes. OBITUARY RULE (binding): the
     2026-09-26 sessions-as-runs note in borge/workstreams.borg ('no
     data.u.<user>.run.* rows ever exist') is superseded FOR SIDEcar RUNS
     IN THE SAME COMMIT that registers this chapter - the session stays the
     TRANSCRIPT record (local JSONL, never tree rows) and the card feed
     stays the human-facing record; the ban on USER-scoped run rows itself
     stands. Sections that phase 2 (supervisor into glochid) and phase 3
     (native dumb runner) implement are marked (PHASE 2)/(PHASE 3).|) *)

 (section run-row
  (doc "STORAGE (RATIFIED): a typed datum in a SHARED space (data.s.run.*)
   keyed by run id - every identity can read the queue, no branch lens can
   fork it (the shared-register precedent: migration 034 moved the Board
   out of data.u.* for the same reason). A dedicated kernel table (the
   jobs shape, migrations/015_jobs.sql) stays the recorded alternative if a
   second supervisor class ever needs structurally-safe claiming (SKIP
   LOCKED; jobs.borg:64-91 records when that trade IS right). CLAIM
   ATOMICITY (RATIFIED, one line NOW, not when a second supervisor class
   appears): the queued -> claimed transition is a COMPARE-AND-SET guarded
   set - flip only a row still queued with no live claim; the reap-takeover
   (resume-semantics 3) is already a two-supervisor moment. Write-cost
   discipline: few bounded rows, text fields capped, transcripts NEVER in
   rows (the 2026-09-08 incident). CHEAP LEASE, EXPENSIVE STATE: a runner
   that dies on wall-clock timeout leaves resumable state in its row.

   FIELDS (the contract; RATIFIED except where noted):
     id          uuid. The run's identity everywhere (evidence, logs,
                 archives).
     card_id     uuid. Linkage only; the card feed stays the human-facing
                 record.
     list_id     uuid. The workstream the card rides.
     from_state  The column the run claimed.
     dest_state  The column this gate advances to (validated against the
                 card's real columns via todo/state/columns-for-list,
                 pp-flow.sh:406-415; empty = one-column advance).
     brief_hash  Hash of the rendered todo/flow-brief at claim time
                 (reproducibility; re-render only on explicit re-brief).
     state       queued | claimed | running | succeeded | failed |
                 timed_out | canceled | dead | parked
     claim       {supervisor_id, lease_ts, heartbeat_at, ttl_s}. ONE
                 supervisor holds it; heartbeat_at bumps every tick while a
                 child lives. Reap rule: heartbeat older than ttl means the
                 supervisor died mid-run; a booting supervisor TAKES THE ROW
                 OVER (the reap_dead pattern, image/lib/jobs.ml:335-347).
                 TTL SHAPE (F5, RATIFIED): ttl_s >= the step's wall_budget_s
                 + 2x tick interval + 60s slack, floor 600s; PROPOSED tick
                 default 30s (roerick ratifies numbers).
     runner      {kind: pp-flow | native, instance_id, identity}.
                 instance_id distinguishes sidecar processes; identity is
                 the runner's OWN identity, never roerick's.
     steps       Ordered list. Each: {name, kind: provider | prim | hand,
                 state: pending | running | done | failed | timed_out |
                 skipped, attempts, max_attempts, wall_budget_s,
                 idempotent, usage: {requests, input_tokens,
                 cached_tokens, output_tokens, wall_s}, evidence_ref,
                 error}.
     budget      {max_total_tokens, max_provider_requests,
                 max_run_wall_s}. Per-step wall lives in each step.
     usage       Accumulated totals, parsed ONLY from executor terminal
                 output - the one place token counts exist today
                 (pp-flow-autolith.sh:223-226 parses :INPUT-TOKENS /
                 :OUTPUT-TOKENS / :PROVIDER-REQUESTS and then DROPS them;
                 this row is where they land).
     evidence    List of {kind: observed | computed | cited | interpreted |
                 asserted, ref, note}. Claim kinds per board card f526bace
                 - the kind is STRUCTURAL, not asserted.
     error       Last failure message (machine-readable class + human
                 text, the :CATEGORY discipline).
     timestamps  created_at, claimed_at, started_at, ended_at.
     acted_by    Per migrations/039_acted_by.sql PINNED semantics: author =
                 owner, acted_by = the runner identity that performed each
                 write.

   IDEMPOTENCY (F1, FINALIZED): a per-step DECLARATION FIELD, not a
   convention. Provider steps default idempotent (a fresh LLM turn is
   naturally retryable); prim/hand steps default NOT idempotent and must be
   checkpoint-first (the with-step rule: resume by CHECKPOINT LOOKUP, not
   code replay - jobs.borg:219) or explicitly declared idempotent. A
   re-dispatch of a non-idempotent, non-checkpointed step is a spec
   violation, not a tuning choice.

   Transitions are owner-confined like jobs (jobs.borg:107-108): only the
   holding supervisor writes state; everyone else reads.")
  (status planned))

 (section lifecycle
  (doc "RUN: queued -> claimed (supervisor takes the lease) -> running
   (step 1 sidecar spawned) -> ... -> succeeded | failed | timed_out |
   canceled | dead | parked.
   STEP: pending -> running -> done | failed | timed_out | skipped.
   RULES:
     - failed WITH attempts remaining -> re-dispatch THE SAME STEP (never
       the whole run).
     - attempts exhausted -> dead. Terminal runs never auto-retry.
     - succeeded -> the supervisor performs the write-back: ONE batched
       habitat eval (card transition + evidence comment + lease release),
       the advance_batch/dest_batch discipline (scripts/pp-flow.sh:191-208).
     - parked -> a human gate or budget exhaustion. Parking posts a card
       comment; parked never auto-retries.
     - THE EXECUTOR NEVER TOUCHES THE BOARD. Sidecar output is scanned by
       the supervisor, which owns every card transition - the 2026-09-26
       exclusive-ownership rule and review independence (one gate = one run
       = one process; workstreams.borg:330-333).
     - A one-way door (prod flips, DNS, deletes) is never a runnable step:
       the run stages it, the run parks, the human gate is a real column.")
  (status planned))

 (section resume-semantics
  (doc "THE FAILURE PATHS - gregor's review leads here; each names the
   mechanism and the finalized contract.
   1. STEP DIES HALFWAY (sidecar crash): step -> failed with its partial
      evidence_ref; the supervisor re-dispatches THE SAME STEP. Idempotency
      is the per-step declaration field (run-row); provider steps retry
      freely; prim/hand steps checkpoint-first or declared idempotent.
   2. RUNNER HANGS: wall budget per step enforced at SPAWN (the
      Io_eio.sh_capture pattern - child killed on timeout,
      image/lib/io_eio.ml:55-57); kill -> timed_out. TWO-LEVEL TIMEOUT
      SEMANTICS, deliberate: adapter-level :TIMED-OUT is terminal for THAT
      ATTEMPT (pp-flow-autolith.sh:147-148 - retrying immediately is the
      same coin flip for the same cost); the supervisor MAY still start a
      FRESH attempt later with a fresh wall. MAX ATTEMPTS (FINALIZED,
      PROPOSED numbers): default 2 attempts for steps whose failure class
      is transient (the adapter's own retry budget); DETERMINISTIC classes
      (:INVALID-*, :PROCESS-FAILURE, 400/401/403/404/422) attempt EXACTLY
      ONCE - a retry can never burn a lease repeatedly on the same
      guaranteed failure. Bounded by POLICY-2 backoff between attempts.
   3. MAYOR RESTARTS MID-PLAN: state lives in the row, not the mayor's
      memory (cheap lease, expensive state). A booting supervisor
      heartbeats its own lease, reaps rows whose claim.heartbeat_at expired
      (TAKES OVER, does not restart), and continues from the step pointer.
      Nothing replays from scratch. This takeover is exactly the
      two-supervisor moment the claim CAS guards.
   4. BUDGET EXHAUSTION: before dispatching any step the supervisor checks
      remaining budget vs the step's estimate; exhaustion ->
      failed(budget) + a card comment + parked. Visible, never silent (the
      no-verdict lesson generalized, pp-flow.sh:649-671). Usage accumulates
      ONLY from executor terminal summaries - a SELF-REPORT (RATIFIED), so
      the budget gate is ADVISORY until provider-structured usage lands.
      The default budget NUMBERS are OPEN on pp-bench FINDINGS (D4 Q7,
      policy-register).
   5. LEASE EXPIRY vs LONG STEPS: claim.heartbeat_at bumps every tick while
      the child process lives; ttl_s > max step wall_budget + tick interval
      + slack (the run-row TTL rule). A step that needs longer gets a
      bigger wall_budget - the lease rule is never ignored. Card-level
      visibility: the card's assignee mirrors the run claim (a
      run:<run-id> shape per the session-lease amendment,
      workstreams.borg:309-323) so humans see the claim on the board.
   6. DETERMINISTIC vs TRANSIENT: the adapter's :CATEGORY rule becomes the
      step-level rule (pp-flow-autolith.sh:130-152): transient provider
      statuses (402/408/409/425/429/5xx) and crash-with-no-result retry;
      deterministic classes fail ONCE.")
  (status planned))

 (section supervisor-tick
  (doc "WHAT THE SUPERVISOR DOES EACH TICK (the glochid app contract;
   PHASE 2):
   1. HEARTBEAT its own supervisor lease; reap run rows whose claim expired
      (take over or fail them).
   2. REAP finished sidecars: parse the terminal output (status, :usage,
      verdict/destination/evidence), accumulate usage into the row, mark
      the step done/failed/timed_out.
   3. ADVANCE: dispatch the next step; on run completion perform the
      write-back (ONE batched eval) or park.
   4. FILL THE QUEUE: cards from todo/item/queue (ORDER owned by the prim,
      workstreams.borg:327-329) minus the eligibility filter (actionable
      states, excluded kinds, no live card lease, no open run row) ->
      create run rows (queued). [POLICY-6 hook: mayor ordering lives here,
      and nowhere else.]
   5. DISPATCH: budget check -> claim (lease + heartbeat) -> spawn the
      sidecar (brief, per-step wall budget, identity env) under the
      concurrency cap (default 2 until the overnight soak passes).
   6. PUBLISH: counts (runs by state, budget burn) for standup v2 to read;
      no new surface in phase 2.

   The sidecar contract stays WHAT EXISTS at first: the supervisor invokes
   scripts/pp-flow.sh --run <card-id> as a child process with env;
   pp-flow's driver logic (claim/brief/write-back) moves INTO the
   supervisor; the adapter (model pinning, envelope, retries, evidence
   archive) stays the sidecar's body until the native runner replaces it
   (phase 3).")
  (status planned))

 (section budget-accounting
  (doc "Budgets live on the run row (max_total_tokens, max_provider_requests,
   max_run_wall_s) and per step (wall_budget_s). Usage accumulates ONLY
   from executor terminal summaries - today that is the adapter's :usage
   parse (pp-flow-autolith.sh:223-226); phase 3's native runner reports
   prim/step counts. USAGE IS A SELF-REPORT (RATIFIED): a crashed or lying
   executor reports zero and the pre-dispatch check sails through - the
   budget gate is ADVISORY until provider-structured usage lands (Autolith
   already holds the real token counts in the provider response and drops
   them at the boundary). PP-side usage_events (docs/monitoring-spec.md:20-29)
   has NO token kinds and is unbuilt; if P1 lands, run-scoped kinds become
   a rollup target, not the enforcement source (D4 Q4). Enforcement is the
   supervisor's pre-dispatch check - no executor self-polices.")
  (status planned))

 (section evidence
  (doc "Evidence entries on the run row carry a CLAIM KIND (observed |
   computed | cited | interpreted | asserted) per board card f526bace, so
   a judge-sweep (the REVIEW gates) can dispatch a probe and say no for a
   stated reason. Phase 2: the kind is a CONVENTION (a required field on
   evidence entries). Phase 3+: enforcement at a claim verb boundary - 'a
   claim verb that REFUSES without a kind' (f526bace tier 2), never by
   overloading tree/put (f526bace). Q3 SCOPE (FINALIZED): the connection is
   EVIDENCE-SIDE only for now - a lease-kind dimension is deferred; revisit
   only when a second lease consumer appears. Card-feed comments remain the
   human-visible evidence (the EVIDENCE: contract, pp-flow.sh:417-433); the
   row is the machine-readable superset.")
  (status planned))

 (section identity
  (doc "ONE IDENTITY PER RUNNER, never roerick's. Phase 2: a revocable
   per-runner Bearer minted via (issue-token ...) - the glochid bridge
   precedent (glochid/lib/habitat.ml:15-20: 'the laptop then holds a
   revocable credential, never the operator's password'). Attribution
   follows migrations/039_acted_by.sql PINNED semantics (author = owner,
   acted_by = the actor; updates COALESCE-preserve) and the FED3 library
   law (the client never sets actor fields, libs/tuna/lib.pp:7-10).
   Allowlists default DENY-ALL; the runner's allowlist names exactly the
   prims it needs (todo read/write, comments) - the agent-native deny-list
   (checkout, forks/merges, promote, auth/session; agent-native.borg:44) is
   the floor. Keys and transcripts live in mode-600 env files, never in
   rows, logs, or chat.")
  (status planned))

 (section policy-register
  (doc "NAMED POLICY DECISIONS - the register of SET / PROPOSED / OPEN.
   Roerick ratifies the PROPOSED numbers at architecture review; nothing
   here blocks phase 2 from starting under the proposed defaults.
   POLICY-1 WORKER-LIFETIME: OPEN (roerick). PROPOSED defaults: per-step
     wall_budget_s 3600s (1h); per-run max_run_wall_s 14400s (4h);
     overridable per card (precedent: PP_FLOW_AUTOLITH_TIMEOUT was raised
     1800s -> 3000s after a real card hit the wall). pp-bench anchor from
     the brief: run 2 = 36 provider requests / 1.75M input tokens / 89%
     cache hits at a 3000s wall.
   POLICY-2 RETRY-COUNTS: SET (gregor) - backoff = the jobs shape, 2^n
     capped 60s (image/lib/jobs.ml:274-290). Per-step max_attempts
     FINALIZED PROPOSED: 2 for transient classes, 1 for deterministic
     (resume-semantics 2/6).
   POLICY-3 REVIEW-GATES: SET (gregor) - the NO-VERDICT fail-safe toward
     the human survives VERBATIM (pp-flow.sh:649-671); which columns
     auto-run a review vs stop for a human stays roerick's call per
     stream.
   POLICY-4 CONCURRENCY-CAPS: SET (gregor, D4 Q9) - default 2 parallel runs
     until the overnight soak passes; per-workstream/per-identity caps are
     roerick's.
   POLICY-5 IDENTITY-POLICY: OPEN (roerick). PROPOSED: per-RUNNER revocable
     Bearer, NOT per-run (per-run minting is a known provisioning gap,
     agent-native.borg:11); rotation = manual per host (tuna-as.sh =
     ~/bin/tuna-as.sh on vultr; the rotation procedure lives off-repo on
     tuna-deriv/club, D4 Q5); allowlist contents per the identity section
     floor.
   POLICY-6 PRIORITY: OPEN (roerick). PROPOSED: the supervisor NEVER
     reorders; the queue ORDER is owned by todo/item/queue (priority, then
     position, then id); the mayor's policy layer is the ONLY place
     ordering may be touched (supervisor-tick 4), and its default is
     PROPOSE-ONLY (it files a card comment suggesting the reorder; a human
     or a future ratified policy applies it).
   Q6 SUPERVISOR RESIDENCE (phase 2, roerick): recommendation recorded -
     wyo's existing glochid systemd service, NO filmstrip dependency
     (register-app! is the UI half and rides filmstrip phase 3; the boot
     gate is already fixed on master, 5b1ba59). Lease-steal policy is
     settled by design: ANY supervisor instance may take over an EXPIRED
     claim (the CAS + TTL rules); a live claim is never stolen.
   Q7 BUDGET DEFAULTS: OPEN - blocked on pp-bench FINDINGS (~/pp-bench
     lives on the vultr/wyo box, not the laptop). Until then POLICY-1's
     proposed numbers apply.")
  (status planned))

 (verify
  (test "a run row created queued, claimed, stepped, and parked survives a supervisor restart and is TAKEN OVER, not restarted"
        "a step killed at its wall budget leaves failed + evidence_ref and re-dispatches the SAME step"
        "the queued -> claimed CAS rejects a second claim on a row that is already claimed or no longer queued"
        "a deterministic-failure step attempts exactly once while a transient one takes max_attempts with jobs-shape backoff"
        "budget exhaustion parks the run with a card comment and no further dispatch"
        "no run row ever contains a transcript or a credential")))
